[Opening "King's Gambit Accepted: MacLeod Defense"]
[StudyName "List of Openings from Beginner to Grandmaster"]
[ChapterName "Kings Gambit (Intermediate  1500-1699)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/2MMWpcNI/gmrKi9Xb"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Lucah873268"]
[UTCDate "2021.03.20"]
[UTCTime "06:00:18"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ This is a VERY risky Gambit. It includes a sacrifice of a pawn. The King's Gambit is a chess opening that begins with the moves: 1. e4 e5 2. f4 White offers a pawn to divert the black e-pawn. If Black accepts the gambit, White has two main plans. The first is to play d4 and Bxf4, regaining the gambit pawn with central domination. Move to e4. } { [%csl Be2] }
1. e4 { [%csl Be4][%cal Be2e4] } 1... e5 { f4 } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gf2f4] } 2. f4 { [%csl Rf4][%cal Re5f4] } 2... exf4 { Move to Knf3 } 3. Nf3 { We sacrifice a Pawn For central domination. It also leaves the f4 pawn weak. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Be4d5,Bf3d4,Bf3e5] } 3... Nc6 { Black wants to develop and abandon the pawn on f4. How can you reinforce the e4 pawn while threatening the f4 pawn? } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Rd2d3] } 4. d3 { Good job! This is the Kings Gambit. And.... You have great control of the center. } { [%csl Rc1,Bd3][%cal Rc1f4,Bd3e4] } *


