[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Accelerated London System"]
[StudyName "List of Openings from Beginner to Grandmaster"]
[ChapterName "London System (Advanced 1700-1900)"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/2MMWpcNI/1m85Q3me"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Lucah873268"]
[UTCDate "2021.03.20"]
[UTCTime "06:11:18"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ This is the London system, it is sort of complicated But it is one of the best Defenses ever. Move to d4. } { [%csl Yd2] }
1. d4 { This move makes the Black Bishop have a better file. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd4e5,Bc1h6] } 1... d5 { Black goes with the same idea. Move to Bf4 } { [%csl Gc1][%cal Rd5e4,Rc8h3] } 2. Bf4 { Now the bishop has even better diagonals. } { [%cal Bf4h2,Bf4b8,Bf4c1,Bf4h6] } 2... Nf6 { Move to e3 } { [%csl Ge2] } 3. e3 { This move enforces the d4 pawns position. And it gives the queen a better diagonal by stopping the move Knh5. } 3... e6 { If Black does not play c5, then move Nd2. } { [%csl Gd2][%cal Gb1d2,Rc7c5] } 4. Nd2 g6 { Next play Bd3 to make way for castling. } { [%csl Gf1,Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 5. Bd3 Bg7 { Now play Nf3 to take control of the e5 and g5 squares, and to castle } { [%cal Gg1f3] } 6. Ngf3 { Black most commonly plays the Zwischenzug c5 before castling. } { [%cal Rc7c5] } 6... c5 { WHENEVER BLACK PLAYS c5, you MUST play c3 to replace the d4 pawn } { [%cal Gc2c3] } 7. c3 cxd4 { [%cal Gc3d4] } 8. cxd4 O-O { Castle } 9. O-O { White has more pieces developed while black has an idle knight and bishop. The rest of the main lines are to attack via bishops. } *


